# Explore Counterfactual Number Theory

Prime numbers combine sparse statistics with rigid multiplication. This notebook separates those roles. Begin with the [harmonic series](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harmonic_series_(mathematics)) and the [Borel–Cantelli lemmas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borel%E2%80%93Cantelli_lemma), then experiment with a multiplicative universe where ordinary composites can become irreducible.

## 1. Follow the probability mass

The benchmark at candidate $n+2$ is $1/\log(n+2)$. Its partial sums grow without bound, even after retaining a fixed arithmetic progression.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the comparison argument</summary>

Since $\log(n+2)\le n+2$, one has $1/(n+2)\le1/\log(n+2)$. The shifted harmonic series diverges. On indices $qn+a$, compare instead with $1/(qn+a+1)$, another divergent harmonic-scale series.

</details>

{{demo:0}}

{{algorithm:0}}

## 2. Generate a random universe

Independent nonsummable event probabilities trigger the second Borel–Cantelli principle: infinitely many selections occur with probability one. Use the widget to see finite realizations fluctuate around their expected count.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the almost-sure argument and its limitation</summary>

Independence bounds the probability of avoiding events from $N$ through $M$ by $\exp(-\sum_{n=N}^M p_n)$, which tends to zero. This proves recurrence, not the sharper asymptotic $X_N\sim N/\log N$.

</details>

{{algorithm:1}}

## 3. Change what factorization means

Now retain only $H=\{n:n\equiv1\pmod4\}$. The numbers $9$, $21$, and $49$ are irreducible there, although composite ordinarily.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal why uniqueness fails</summary>

All four displayed factors are irreducible in $H$, yet $441=9\cdot49=21\cdot21$. The factor multisets differ. Infinitely many irreducibles nevertheless remain because every ordinary prime congruent to $1$ modulo $4$ is irreducible in $H$.

</details>

{{algorithm:2}}

## 4. Where the analogy stops

A full random prime number theorem still needs concentration and expectation asymptotics. A random Riemann hypothesis needs a specifically defined random Dirichlet series or Euler product, convergence, and analytic continuation. Explore background on the [prime number theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_number_theorem) and [Riemann zeta function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_zeta_function), keeping in mind that nonunique factorization blocks a naive Euler product in the Hilbert universe.

